From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Juan Pablo Lassala <jpablolassala@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 8 Oct 2026 11:45:00 -0300
Subject: [PATCH] translator: Keep a menu open while Firefox makes its menubar
 again

Firefox serves its menubar as a dbusmenu, and makes the whole of it again,
every item with a new id, whenever one of its menus changes. That is many
times a minute while a page loads, and each time some of its menus are
told they were opened: History, Bookmarks, New Container Tab.

The translator replaced every item whose id changed, and after any layout
it said that all of the menu had changed. So each time, the menubar on
screen was made again, which closed the submenu the user had open. The
pointer resting on its item opened it again, Firefox was told "closed" and
"opened", and made its menubar again: the menu opened and closed several
times a second. And each new item's submenu was preloaded, which sends
"opened" too, so with a Firefox window focused the two sent each other
hundreds of messages a second with no menu open.

Let a submenu that only has a new id stay the item it was. Its action
keeps its name, which has the old id, and finds the id to use on its
model, as it did. The submenu gets its layout again and is not preloaded
again.

Say that a menu changed only if it did.

And tell the application that a submenu was closed half a second late,
and not at all if it is opened again before: for the application it was
open all along. An item that is not a submenu is still replaced when its
id changes, which makes the items of an open menu again and closes a
submenu of it.

An application that keeps its items' ids, as Qt's do, hears "closed" half
a second later than before, and its menus are no longer made again on
screen by a layout that changes nothing.
